  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 258 AM EDT Wednesday...\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1: Surface high pressure cresting overhead this \nmorning will provide the region with a seasonable and dry day \nwith partly sunny skies. Clouds will increase tonight ahead of \nnext approaching system. A surface low pressure system will \ntrack from the Great Lakes area northeastward into Ontario and \nthen Quebec. Series of frontal passages on Thursday into \nThursday night will bring chances for showers and thunderstorms \nto our area. SPC has placed our region in Marginal risk for \nsevere thunderstorms on Thursday. Moderate instability is \nexpected ahead of a cold frontal passage as some higher \ndewpoints advect into our area on south southwesterly flow. Some\nmodest shear may help some storms to become better organized \nwith some strong winds and small hail possible. Areas further \nsouth of our forecast area will have better chances for severe \nstorms. Additional showers are expected Friday as upper level \ntrough trails behind departing surface fronts and crosses our \narea. Severe weather is not expected, though embedded \nthunderstorms will be possible.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2: Following a mainly dry weekend, there still \nlooks to be some signs of subtle cyclonic flow as an upper low \nlifts north and east, which may spur some isolated shower \nactivity in eastern Ontario and southern Quebec late Sunday. \nSome of which will bring some increase cloud cover and an \nisolated shower to the St. Lawrence Valley and areas near the \nInternational Border. Our flow pattern will gradually become \nmore zonal towards late Sunday into Monday, favoring more hit or\nmiss showers in the aforementioned areas. Temperatures will \nbegin to increase above seasonable norms by 3-5 degrees, Sunday \ninto Monday as a thermal ridge moves through the region. Highs \nSunday into early next week will be in the upper 70s for the \nhigher terrain, and low 80s in the wider valleys, which is \nsupported by 850mb temperatures of 16-19C. Humidity will also be\non the incline, peaking Monday in the low to mid 60s. The \nincrease in humidity will be a result of a trough swinging \nthrough the area to start next week. Models are beginning to \npick up on a weak shortwave that will ride through the thermal \nridge Monday into Tuesday. Shower chances will increase by early\nMonday through Tuesday across the region. While ingredients \nwill be marginal Monday, some thunder chances will be possible \nMonday afternoon driven by a weak frontal boundary passage. \nThunder chances will also be possible Tuesday, but will be \nconditional on the frontal passage on Monday. A slower front \nMonday would help spur thunder chances, and perhaps some \nstronger storms Tuesday as storm chances would be associated \nwith better instability and shear profiles. A faster front \nMonday would lower thunder chances Tuesday. While the pattern is\nrelatively unsettled early next week, precipitation \nprobabilities are generally low, with only a 20-40% chance for \n0.5\" highlighted in the northern portions of our area Monday \ninto Tuesday in the GFS ensembles and AI suite.",
